  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nWeak midlevel ridging will be in place across South Texas through \nthe weekend, maintaining generally warm and dry conditions with \nabove-normal temperatures. Subsidence associated with the ridge \nshould suppress most convective development today and tomorrow. \nHowever, increasing low-level moisture beginning Sunday may support \na few showers to kick off the work week, primarily across the \ncoastal waters and eastern portions of the CWA. Otherwise, \ntemperatures will remain above normal with generally light to \nmoderate onshore flow.\n\nAs a surge of deeper tropical moisture is expected to spread across \nSouth Texas early in the week, GEFS ensemble mean PWAT values are \nexpected to increase to around 2.2-2.3 inches, approaching or \nexceeding 2 standard deviations above climatological norms. Despite \nthe anomalously high moisture content, forcing for ascent appears \nrather limited as weak midlevel ridging persists across the region. \nLittle appreciable midlevel PVA is indicated by the GFS, while \nsurface convergence should remain largely confined to the sea breeze \nwith a potential boundary just offshore. Nevertheless, the moisture-\nrich environment may be sufficient to support a low (20-35%) chance \nof isolated to scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms, \nparticularly across the eastern half of the CWA and adjacent coastal \nwaters. Although, coverage may be limited by the lack of stronger \nforcing.\n\nRain chances should diminish late Monday while temperatures remain \nelevated through much of the workweek. Forecast confidence decreases \nconsiderably the second half of the week as deterministic guidance \ndiverges significantly regarding the evolution of a potentially \nstrong cold front. The GFS depicts a substantial southward surge of \ncolder air, bringing a frontal passage through South Texas next \nweekend, while the Euro keeps the boundary well north of the region. \nGEFS temperature anomalies lend some support to a cooler pattern \ndeveloping across much of Texas, although considerable uncertainty \nremains regarding the progression of the front and how far south the \ncolder air will penetrate. Given these differences, confidence \nremains low. For now, will maintain a more conservative temperature \nand precipitation forecast while monitoring subsequent model and \nensemble trends for greater consistency. \n\nMinor coastal flooding is expected to continue to be a threat at \nleast for the next few days as tides remain elevated, but this \nshould gradually diminish with the seas as P-ETSS guidance suggests \nwater levels could remain at or near 2.0 feet MSL through\nWednesday.",
